# Per-project ORCHESTRATOR settings. Copy to projects/<name>/symphony.env — it
# is gitignored.
#
# Read AFTER the root symphony.env (last value wins) and passed to compose
# via --env-file only — this file is NEVER named by an `env_file:` directive
# anywhere in this stack. That is the mechanism that keeps this token out of
# the agent's environment, not a filing convention: the agent's container
# gets .env and projects/<name>/.env wholesale, and this value is in neither.
#
# This file is also never mounted into the symphony container itself — only
# projects/<name>/config/ is (read-only, as WORKFLOW.md). `symphony check`
# refuses to start if an agent env file is readable from inside the
# orchestrator's mount, and the reverse holds here by the same logic: this
# credential file has no path into that mount either.

# Only for tracker.kind: gitlab (set in this project's config/WORKFLOW.md).
# Leave blank for the file queue and this container keeps its stronger
# posture: no credentials and no egress at all.
#
# A PROJECT access token, REPORTER role, `api` scope. It reads and writes
# issues on this one project and CANNOT push code — that's the agent's
# Developer token (GITLAB_PAT, in this project's .env), a different token in
# a different container. `api` is full API access for that project; there is
# no issues-only scope. The ROLE is what constrains it, not the scope — get
# the role right.
#
# Leave this commented out to inherit SYMPHONY_GITLAB_TOKEN from the shared
# symphony.env instead — that's what a GROUP access token over a sandbox
# group is for: one credential covering several projects. `symphony check`
# reports which of the two a project is actually running with, so it is
# never silent either way.
# SYMPHONY_GITLAB_TOKEN=
